ntal gap, you should step the line out away from the node before turning.\n\n#### Step 1: Improve the \"Step-out\" Logic\nModify `buildObstacleFreePath` to always \"step out\" vertically from the source and destination before attempting to travel horizontally.\n\n```kotlin\n// Replace the start of your buildObstacleFreePath logic\nif (!isBackEdge) {\n    val stepOutY \u003d 20f // Distance to move away from node before turning\n    val sourceExitPoint \u003d Offset(start.x, start.y + stepOutY)\n    waypoints.add(sourceExitPoint)\n\n    // Now find a corridor starting FROM the exit point\n    val corridorY \u003d findSafeHorizontalCorridor(\n        start.x, approach.x, sourceExitPoint.y, approach.y,\n        fromOrganName, toOrganName, nodePositions\n    )\n    \n    waypoints.add(Offset(start.x, corridorY))\n    waypoints.add(Offset(approach.x, corridorY))\n    waypoints.add(approach)\n}\n```\n\n#### Step 2: Add Vertical Obstacle Checking\nYour `findSafeHorizontalCorridor` needs to ensure the \"entry\" and \"exit\" vertical segments are also clear. \n\n```kotlin\nprivate fun isPathClear(\n    p1: Offset, p2: Offset, \n    ignored1: String, ignored2: String, \n    nodes: Map\u003cString, NodePosition\u003e\n): Boolean {\n    for ((name, pos) in nodes) {\n        if (name \u003d\u003d ignored1 || name \u003d\u003d ignored2) continue\n        if (lineSegmentIntersectsNode(p1.x, p1.y, p2.x, p2.y, pos, margin \u003d 15f)) {\n            return false\n        }\n    }\n    return true\n}\n\n// In your routing logic, check ALL three segments:\nval seg1 \u003d isPathClear(sourceExitPoint, Offset(start.x, testY), ...)\nval seg2 \u003d isPathClear(Offset(start.x, testY), Offset(approach.x, testY), ...)\nval seg3 \u003d isPathClear(Offset(approach.x, testY), approach, ...)\n\nif (seg1 \u0026\u0026 seg2 \u0026\u0026 seg3) return testY // This Y is truly safe\n```\n\n#### Step 3: Implement \"S-Curve\" Routing for Vertical Alignment\nIf two nodes are in the same column (same X), your current code tries to draw a straight line. If a node is between them, `pathHasObstacles` returns true, but your corridor logic might still fail.\n\n**The Fix:** If the path is blocked vertically, force the line to route horizontally into the \"gutter\" (the space between columns), travel down, and then route back in.\n\n```kotlin\nif (kotlin.math.abs(approach.x - start.x) \u003c 5f) {\n    if (pathHasObstacles(start, approach, fromOrganName, toOrganName, nodePositions)) {\n        // Force an \"S\" shape to the right gutter\n        val gutterX \u003d start.x + (NODE_WIDTH / 2) + (HORIZONTAL_NODE_SPACING / 2)\n        waypoints.add(Offset(start.x, start.y + 20f)) // Step down\n        waypoints.add(Offset(gutterX, start.y + 20f)) // Step into gutter\n        waypoints.add(Offset(gutterX, approach.y))    // Travel down gutter\n        waypoints.add(approach)                       // Back to approach\n        waypoints.add(end)\n        return waypoints\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Summary of why it\u0027s happening:\n1. **Vertical collision neglect:** You check if the horizontal \"bridge\" is safe, but not the vertical \"pillars\" connecting to it.\n2. **Same-Column conflict:** When nodes are in the same column, the straight path is blocked, and the \"corridor\" search doesn\u0027t know how to move the line *sideways* into the empty space between columns to bypass the obstacle.",
